call_details.response:
The board is interpreted with the first input line as row 0 (top) and the last input line as row 5 (bottom). Column heights are therefore 2, 5, 4, 6, 4, 4, 2 respectively, so the only legal columns are 0, 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6.

No placement of \(\mathrm{o}\) produces four consecutive \(\mathrm{o}\) tokens:
- column 0 (row 3) yields at most three horizontal \(\mathrm{o}\);
- column 1 (row 0) yields at most two vertical \(\mathrm{o}\);
- column 2 (row 1) yields at most two tokens in any direction;
- column 4 (row 1) yields at most two tokens in any direction;
- column 5 (row 1) yields at most two tokens in any direction;
- column 6 (row 3) yields exactly three diagonal \(\mathrm{o}\).

Consequently there is no immediate win.  

The same exhaustive check for \(\mathrm{x}\) shows that a placement in column 6 (row 3) completes four horizontal \(\mathrm{x}\) tokens on row 3 (columns 3-6). No other legal column produces four consecutive \(\mathrm{x}\).  

If column 6 is left open, \(\mathrm{x}\) wins on the next turn. The unique move that prevents an immediate loss is therefore column 6.

Final Answer: 6

